{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S15ZVX32J7tc7jknREJ6iH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n2\t// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n3\t\n4\tpackage sys\n5\t\n6\timport (\n7\t\t\"os\"\n8\t\t\"os/signal\"\n9\t\t\"syscall\"\n10\t)\n11\t\n12\t// InstallSIGPIPEHandler installs a SIGPIPE handler that causes the process to\n13\t// exit 0 when stdout or stderr is closed by a downstream consumer, matching\n14\t// GNU coreutils behavior.\n15\t// R1.5: uses signal.Notify with a buffered channel; does not use signal.Ignore.\n16\t// R1.6: safe to call multiple times; each invocation starts one goroutine.\n17\tfunc InstallSIGPIPEHandler() {\n18\t\tc := make(chan os.Signal, 1)\n19\t\tsignal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE)\n20\t\tgo func() {\n21\t\t\t<-c\n22\t\t\tos.Exit(0)\n23\t\t}()\n24\t}\n25\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HfRRCAWT4W954bQkePgNDG","session_id":"f7039a9e-9225-44ca-a28d-bff949cf7958","uuid":"a1fb3f3b-6912-486c-9ba4-ec4b360c63df","timestamp":"2026-05-14T17:33:39.868Z"}
