{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01X3sSvV3NApkf5sYCb1hQUj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have everything I need to implement cmd/uname/main.go. Let me design it:\n\n1. Use `golang.org/x/sys/unix` for `unix.Uname` and `unix.Utsname`\n2. Convert `[256]byte` arrays to strings by finding null terminator\n3. Parse short flags manually (POSIX-style combined flags like -snr)\n4. Handle --help, --version, --, error cases\n5. Default (no flags) = print kernel name (sysname)\n6. Extensible structure for future flags\n\nKey behaviors from guname:\n\n\n- Default prints just sysname, but combined flags like -snr output space-separated values in canonical order\n- Invalid flags and extra operands trigger specific error messages with exit code 1\n- The -a flag would output all fields in a defined sequence: sysname, nodename, release, version, machine, processor, hardware platform, operating system\n\nNow I'm planning the implementation structure: I'll use a helper to convert byte arrays to strings, track which flags were requested in a set, then output the corresponding fields in canonical order. For parsing, I need to handle combined short flags like -snr as individual flags, recognize special cases like --help and --, and catch any remaining arguments as operands that should error out. I'm keeping functions under 40 lines and focusing on the core requirements first while building something extensible for future flags.\n\nWriting the Go implementation... I'm thinking through the canonical ordering for the `-a` flag, which should follow the POSIX standard: sysname, nodename, release, version, machine, processor, hardware platform, and operating system. Right now I only need to handle s, n, and r, but I want to structure the code so adding v, m, p, i, and o later is straightforward—just adding entries to a slice.\n\nI should also decide between `syscall.Uname` and `golang.org/x/sys/unix`. Since `syscall.Uname` doesn't exist on Darwin and the project already has `golang.org/x/sys v0.44.0` in go.mod, I'll use that instead.\n\nNow I'm refining the structure to keep functions under 40 lines.\n\nWriting the main function... Writing the Go implementation...\n\nI need to reconsider the help text since the spec says we shouldn't support long-form flags beyond --help and --version, so I should remove those long options from the usage output. Let me check what the actual GNU uname help looks like to match the expected format.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":180,"cache_read_input_tokens":29942,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":180},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f7039a9e-9225-44ca-a28d-bff949cf7958","uuid":"faac2e89-e4b4-4344-881d-4b884d09d113"}
